For a grounding criterion, start your reason by quoting the exact words in the draft whose support is weakest. Compare their scope to an exact phrase in the cited passage. Do not silently simplify the sentence: dropping an adverb, qualifier, timeframe or denominator changes the claim. If occurrence is documented but its frequency is unknown, a claim of frequency fails even with an accurate caveat elsewhere.
